Also, denoting by $p_X$ the density function of $X$,
\[ \ent{X} = \sum_x p_X(x) \log \frac{1}{p_X(x)} \geqslant \min_{x : p_X(x)>0} \log \frac{1}{p_X(x)},\] and therefore
\begin{equation}\label{px-lower} \max_x p_X(x) \geqslant e^{-\ent{X}}.\end{equation}

The \emph{mutual information} $\I{X:Y}$ is defined by the formula
\[
  \begin{split}
    \I{X:Y} &= \ent{X} + \ent{Y} - \ent{X,Y} \\
            &= \ent{X} - \ent{X|Y} \\
            &= \ent{Y} - \ent{Y|X},
  \end{split}
\]
and is non-negative by a standard application of Jensen's inequality, vanishing precisely when $X,Y$ are independent; in particular
\begin{equation}
\label{indep}
  \ent{X,Y} = \ent{X} + \ent{Y}
\end{equation}
if and only if $X,Y$ are independent, and
\begin{equation}\label{cond-dec} \ent{X | Y} \leqslant \ent{X}\end{equation} always.

Whenever $X,Y$ are $G$-valued random variables, we have
that
\[
  \ent{X\pm Y} \geqslant \ent{X\pm Y|Y} = \ent{X|Y} = \ent{X} - \I{X:Y}
\]
and similarly with the roles of $X,Y$ reversed, thus
\begin{equation}
  \label{sumset-lower-gen}
  \max(\ent{X}, \ent{Y}) - \I{X:Y} \leqslant \ent{X\pm Y}.
\end{equation}

A particular consequence of~\eqref{sumset-lower-gen} is that
\begin{equation}
  \label{sumset-lower}
  \max(\ent{X}, \ent{Y}) \leqslant \ent{X\pm Y}
\end{equation}
when $X,Y$ are independent.

